    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d428">

                <pb facs="1179" n="1163" sameAs="frus:frus1951v01/pg1163"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">460.509/7–3151: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gifford</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-07-31T20:00:00+01:00">July 31, 1951—8 p.
                            m</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>638. Excon. For Linder. In view of the consultative group meeting in Paris
                    tomorrow to consider our proposals for strengthening security <pb facs="1180"
                        n="1164" xml:id="pg1164"/> controls on exports to the Soviet bloc, I saw
                        Morrison<anchor n="1" xml:id="d428-f1"/> and expressed to him the hope that
                    the Brit Govt would do everything possible to reduce to the minimum the
                    remaining area of disagreement between US on the controls. I hoped that they
                    would be able to add as many as possible to the 20 items which we had proposed
                    for list I and to which they had not yet agreed. I pointed out the importance of
                    the Brit position since other Western European countries tend to follow their
                    lead. Morrison emphasized again their need for maintaining some trade with the
                    East in order to live, mentioning particularly possible short supplies of meat
                    and the need for grain for feedstuffs, but agreed to do what he could.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept 638, repeated info Paris 235.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gifford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d428-f1" n="1" xml:id="d428-f1-content">
                <p>Herbert Morrison, British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
